          %h2#Idea Idea
          %p
            In full
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+logic"} linear logic
            \&amp;lbrack;
            %a{:href => "#Girard1987"}> Girard 1987
            \&amp;rbrack; and more generally in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+type+theory"} linear type theory
            there is assumed a (comonadic)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modality"} modality
            traditionally denoted “!” whose role is to model the “underlying” classical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intuitionistic+type+theory"} (intuitionistic) type
            of a given
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+type"} linear type
          %p
            This is alternatively called the “exponential modality” (for good reasons discussed
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#TheExponentialCondition"} below
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+logic"} modal logic
          %p
            In classical linear logic (meaning with involutive
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Morgan+duality"} de Morgan duality
          %p
            Today it is understood (cf.
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Melliès09"} Melliès 2009
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "/nlab/files/Mellies-CategoricalSemanticsLinear.pdf#page=36"} p. 36
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comonad"} comonad
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+types"} linear types
            (
            %a{:href => "#Seely89"}> Seely 1989 §2
            ,
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#dePaiva89"} dePaiva 1989
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#BBPH92"} Benton, Bierman, de Paiva and Hyland 1992
            %a{:href => "monad#RelationBetweenAdjunctionsAndMonads"} induced
            by an adjunction to classical (meaning here: non-linear
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intuitionistic+type+theory"} intuitionistic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+functor"} monoidal
            properties (
            %a{:href => "#Seely89"}> Seely 1989 §2
            ,
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Bierman94"} Bierman 1994 pp. 157
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Benton95"} Benton 1995
          %p
            In a most elementary and illuminating example (which may not have received the attention it deserves, cf. the history at
            = succeed "):" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+logic"} quantum logic
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                classical intuitionistic types form (are interpreted in) the category of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sets"} Sets
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ClaTypes
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo ≔
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi Set
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ClaTypes \,\coloneqq\, Set
            %li
              %p
                linear types form (are interpreted in) the category of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+spaces"} vector spaces
                (cf.
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "linear+logic#Murfet14"} Murfet 2014
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi LinTypes
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo ≔
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %msub
                        %mi Vect
                        %mi 𝕂
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} LinTypes  \,\coloneqq\, Vect_{\mathbb{K}}
          %p
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functors"} adjoint functors
            in question are
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                forming the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+span"} linear span
                of a set
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mrow
                        %mtable
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %mi Set
                            %mtd
                              %mover
                                %mo ⟶
                                %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Q
                            %mtd
                              %mi Vect
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %mi W
                            %mtd
                              %mo ↦
                            %mtd
                              %msub
                                %mo ⊕
                                %mi W
                              %mi 𝟙
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\array{
                      Set &amp;\overset{\mathrm{Q}}{\longrightarrow}&amp; Vect
                      \\\
                      W &amp;\mapsto&amp; \oplus_W \mathbb{1}
                      }
            %li
              %p
                forming the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/underlying+set"} underlying set
                of a
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+space"} vector space
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mrow
                        %mtable
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %mi Vect
                            %mtd
                              %mover
                                %mo ⟶
                                %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} C
                            %mtd
                              %mi Set
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %mi.mathscript 𝒱
                            %mtd
                              %mo ↦
                            %mtd
                              %mi Hom
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi 𝟙
                              %mo ,
                              %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                              %mi.mathscript 𝒱
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\array{
                      Vect &amp;\overset{\mathrm{C}}{\longrightarrow}&amp; Set
                      \\\
                      \\mathscr{V} &amp;\mapsto&amp; Hom(\mathbb{1}, \, \mathscr{V})
                      }
          %p in which case the exponential modality acts by sending a vector space to the linear span of its underlying set
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi Vect
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo ⟶
                            %mrow
                              %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                              %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                              %mo !
                              %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                              %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mtd
                          %mi Vect
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi.mathscript 𝒱
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↦
                        %mtd
                          %munder
                            %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⨁
                            %mi.mathscript 𝒱
                          %mi 𝟙
                          %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                          %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  Vect &amp;\overset{\;\; ! \;\;}{\longrightarrow}&amp; Vect
                  \\\
                  \\mathscr{V} &amp;\mapsto&amp; \underset{\mathscr{V}}{\bigoplus} \mathbb{1}
                  \\,.
                  }
          %p
            One sees that this construction takes (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+sums"}> direct
            ) sums to (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+of+vector+spaces"}> tensor
            ) products
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo !
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi.mathscript 𝒱
                  %mo ⊕
                  %mi.mathscript 𝒱
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ≃
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo !
                  %mi.mathscript 𝒱
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo !
                  %mi.mathscript 𝒱
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \!(\mathscr{V} \oplus \mathscr{V}')
                  \\;\simeq\;
                  (! \mathscr{V}) \otimes (! \mathscr{V}')
          %p
            and zero (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/zero+object"}> objects
            ) to unit (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit+object"}> objects
            )
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo !
                  %mn 0
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ≃
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi 𝟙
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \! 0
                  \\;\simeq\;
                  \\mathbb{1}
          %p
            as expected of any
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exponential+map"} exponential map
          %p
            This example is the 0-sector of the more famous
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stabilization"} stabilization
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C1%29-adjoint+functor"}>
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
              \-
            )adjunction
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo +
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo ⊣
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mo +
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\Sigma_+^\infty \dashv \Omega_+^\infty)
            between (the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            of)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
            and (the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+homotopy+theory"} stable homotopy theory
            of)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/module+spectra"} module
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectra"} spectra
            equipped with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+smash+product+of+spectra"} symmetric smash product of spectra
            (given by forming
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/suspension+spectra"} suspension spectra
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ↦
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Σ
                      %mo +
                      %mn ∞
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \mapsto \Sigma_+^\infty X
            %a{:href => "#RealizationInLinearHomotopyTypeTheory"} below
            and at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+homotopy+type+theory"} linear homotopy type theory
            for more on this.
          %center
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/QuantClassExponential-230821.jpg", :width => "600"}/
          %h2#categorical_semantics Categorical semantics
          %p
            Following
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Seely89"} Seely 1989
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#dePaiva89"} dePaiva 1989
            %a{:href => "#BBPH92"} Benton, Bierman, de Paiva and Hyland 1992
            it is common convention that “!” should be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comonad"} comonad
            on the type system (and ? should be a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad"} monad
            = succeed ")," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monads+in+computer+science"} monads in computer science
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo !
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} !A
            to be duplicated and discarded.
          %h3#intuitionistic_case Intuitionistic case
          %p
            The definition from
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Seely89"} Seely
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi LinTypes
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} LinTypes
              be
            %ul
              %li
                a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+monoidal+category"} cartesian monoidal category
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+product"} Cartesian product
                “
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ×
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \times
                ” and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+unit"} tensor unit
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal object
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo *
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast
            %p
              which
              %em in addition
              carries the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure"} structure
              of
            %ul
              %li
                a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal category
                with respect to a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊗
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \otimes
                with
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+unit"} tensor unit
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝟙
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{1}
            %p
              A
              %strong Seely comonad
              on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi LinTypes
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} LinTypes
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comonad"} comonad
              that is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+monoidal+functor"} strong monoidal
              as a functor from cartesian monoidal structure
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ×
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \times
              to the other monoidal structure
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊗
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \otimes
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi LinTypes
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ×
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mo !
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi LinTypes
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (LinTypes, \times)
                    \\xrightarrow{ \; ! \; }
                    (LinTypes, \otimes)
            %p
              meaning that there are
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformations"} natural transformations
              of the form
            #eq:StrongMonoidalPropertyOfExponential.maruku-equation
              %span.maruku-eq-number (1)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi LinTypes
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo !
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ×
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ≅
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo !
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo !
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    A,\, B \colon LinTypes
                    \\;\;\;\;\;\;\;
                    \\vdash
                    \\;\;\;\;\;\;\;
                    \!(A \times B)
                    \\;\cong\;
                    (!A) \otimes (!B)
            %p and
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo !
                    %mo *
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ≃
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi 𝟙
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \! \ast \;\;\simeq\;\; \mathbb{1}
                    \\,.
          %p
            (There is also an additional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherence"} coherence
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom"} axiom
            that should be imposed; see
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a{:href => "#Melliès09"} Melliès 2009, section 7.3
          %p
          #TheExponentialCondition.num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              %strong (the exponential condition)
              %br/
              In
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+logic"} linear logic
              = succeed "\u201D" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi &amp;
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \&amp;
              = succeed "\u201D)," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/additive+conjunction"} additive conjunction
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:StrongMonoidalPropertyOfExponential"} (1)
              reads
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo !
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mi &amp;
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ≅
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo !
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo !
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \!(A \&amp; B)
                    \\;\cong\;
                    (!A) \otimes (!B)
                    \\,.
            %p
              But in key examples of categories of (the multiplicative fragment of) linear logic (such as
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vect"} Vect
              = succeed ")," do
                %a{:href => "linear+logic#Murfet14"} Murfet 2014
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+product"} cartesian product
              is actually a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/biproduct"} biproduct
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+sum"} direct sum
            %p
              %em it takes (direct) sums to (tensor) products
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo !
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊕
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ≅
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo !
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo !
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \!(A \oplus B)
                    \\;\cong\;
                    (!A) \otimes (!B)
            %p
              %em it takes zero (objects) to unit (objects)
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo !
                    %mn 0
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ≅
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi 𝟙
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \! 0
                    \\;\cong\;
                    \\mathbb{1}
            %p
              as befits any
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exponential+map"} exponential map
              and may explain the choice of terminology here.
          %p
            This condition implies that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kleisli+category+of+a+comonad"} Kleisli category
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo !
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} !
            (i.e. the category of cofree !-coalgebras) is cartesian monoidal. If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi LinTypes
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} LinTypes
            is
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+monoidal+category"} closed
            symmetric monoidal then the Kleisli category of a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+closed+category"} cartesian closed category
          %p
            Of course, the above definition depends on the existence of the cartesian product. A different definition that doesn’t require the existence of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ×
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \times
            was given by
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "#BBPH92"} Benton, Bierman, de Paiva, and Hyland
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi LinTypes
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} LinTypes
              be a
              = succeed ";" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal category
              %strong linear exponential comonad
              on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi LinTypes
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} LinTypes
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lax+monoidal+functor"} lax monoidal
              comonad such that every cofree !-coalgebra naturally carries the structure of a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comonoid+object"} comonoid object
              in the category of coalgebras (i.e. the cofree-coalgebra functor lifts to the category of comonoids in the category of coalgebras).
          %p
            It follows automatically that all !-coalgebras are comonoids, and therefore that the category of all !-coalgebras (not just the cofree ones) is cartesian monoidal. Note that for a comonad on a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset"} poset
          %p
            A more even-handed approach is the following (see
            %a{:href => "#Benton95"} Benton (1995)
            and
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#Melliès09"} Melliès (2009)
          %p
          #LinearNonlinearAdjunction.num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
            %p
              A
              %strong linear-nonlinear adjunction
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+adjunction"} monoidal adjunction
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi ClaTypes
                    %mo ⇄
                    %mi LinTypes
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F \colon ClaTypes \rightleftarrows LinTypes \colon G
              in which
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi LinTypes
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} LinTypes
              is symmetric monoidal and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ClaTypes
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ClaTypes
              is cartesian monoidal. The induced !-modality is the induced
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comonad"} comonad
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F G
              on
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi LinTypes
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} LinTypes
            %p
          %p
            This includes both of the previous definitions where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            is taken respectively to be the Kleisli category or the Eilenberg-Moore category of !. Conversely, in any linear-nonlinear adjunction the induced comonad
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F G
            can be shown to be a linear exponential comonad. Moreover, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo !
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} !
            is a linear exponential comonad on a symmetric monoidal category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi LinTypes
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} LinTypes
            with finite products, then the cofree !-coalgebra functor is a right adjoint and hence preserves cartesian products; but the cartesian products of coalgebras are the tensor products in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo !
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ×
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≅
                    %mo !
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mo !
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} !(A\times B) \cong !A \otimes !B
          %h3#classical_case Classical case
          %p
            For “classical” linear logic, we want
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi LinTypes
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} LinTypes
            to be not just (closed) symmetric monoidal but
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/star-autonomous+category"} autonomous
            = succeed "-autonomous" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo !
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} !
            one can derive a ? from the ! by
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Morgan+duality"} de Morgan duality
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ?
                    %mi A
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                    %mo !
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi A
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msup
                      %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ?A = \big(!(A^*)\big)^*
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "#BluteCockettSeely96"} Blute, Cockett &amp; Seely (1996)
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi LinTypes
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} LinTypes
              be a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linearly+distributive+category"} linearly distributive category
              with tensor product
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊗
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \otimes
              and cotensor product
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⅋
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \parr
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi LinTypes
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} LinTypes
              consists of:
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  a
                  = succeed "-monoidal" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo ⊗
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \otimes
                  = succeed "-comonoidal" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo ⅋
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \parr
              %li
                %p ? is a !-strong monad, and ! is a ?-strong comonad
              %li
                %p
                  all free !-coalgebras are naturally commutative
                  = succeed "-comonoids," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo ⊗
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \otimes
                  = succeed "-monoids." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo ⅋
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \parr
          %p
            Here a functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+functor"} strong
            with respect to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lax+monoidal+functor"} lax monoidal functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            if there is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformation"} natural transformation
            of the form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi G
                  %mi B
                  %mo →
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi G
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F A \otimes G B \to F(A\otimes G B)
            satisfying some natural axioms, and we similarly require compatibility of the monad and comonad structure transformations.
            %a{:href => "#BluteCockettSeely96"} BCS96
            showed that if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi LinTypes
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} LinTypes
            is in fact
            = succeed "-autonomous," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ?
                    %mi A
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                    %mo !
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi A
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msup
                      %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ?A = \big(!(A^*)\big)^*
          %h2#examples Examples
          %h3#relation_to_chu_construction Relation to Chu construction
          %p
          .num_theorem
            %h6 Theorem
            %p
              Suppose
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi M
                    %mo ⇄
                    %mi C
                    %mo :
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F \colon M \rightleftarrows C : G
              is a linear-nonlinear adjunction (Def.
              = succeed ")," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#LinearNonlinearAdjunction"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              is closed symmetric monoidal with finite limits and colimits, and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊥
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bot\in C
              is an object. Then there is an induced linear-nonlinear adjunction
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                    %mo ⇄
                    %mi Chu
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ⊥
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M \rightleftarrows Chu(C,\bot)
              where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Chu
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ⊥
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Chu(C,\bot)
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chu+construction"} Chu construction
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Chu
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi C
                      %mo ,
                      %mo ⊥
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Chu(C,\bot)
              = succeed "-autonomous" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Chu
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ⊥
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Chu(C,\bot)
              admits a !-modality.
          .proof
            %h6 Proof
            %p
              The embedding of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Chu
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ⊥
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Chu(C,\bot)
              as
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ↦
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ⊥
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ev
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \mapsto (A, [A,\bot], ev)
              is coreflective: the coreflection of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi B
                      %mo +
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi B
                      %mo −
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi e
                      %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (B^+, B^-, e_B)
              is
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mi B
                        %mo +
                      %mo ,
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %msup
                        %mi B
                        %mo +
                      %mo ,
                      %mo ⊥
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo ,
                      %mi ev
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (B^+, [B^+,\bot], ev)
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Chu
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi C
                      %mo ,
                      %mo ⊥
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Chu(C,\bot)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi Chu
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ⊥
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C\hookrightarrow Chu(C,\bot)
              is strong monoidal, hence the adjunction is a monoidal adjunction. Therefore, the composite adjunction
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                    %mo ⇄
                    %mi C
                    %mo ⇄
                    %mi Chu
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ⊥
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M \rightleftarrows C \rightleftarrows Chu(C,\bot)
              is again a linear-nonlinear-adjunction.
          %p
            Note that this theorem is a direct consequence of the work of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yves+Lafont"} Yves Lafont
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thomas+Streicher"} Thomas Streicher
            on game semantics using the Chu construction, together with Benton’s reformulation of linear logic in terms of monoidal adjunctions. Since a Chu construction is
            = succeed "-autonomous," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast
          %p
          .num_cor
            %h6 Corollary
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              is a cartesian closed category with finite limits and colimits and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊥
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bot\in C
              is an object, then there is a linear-nonlinear adjunction
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                      %mo ⇄
                      %mi Chu
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi C
                      %mo ,
                      %mo ⊥
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \rightleftarrows Chu(C,\bot)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Chu
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ⊥
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Chu(C,\bot)
              admits a !-modality.
          .proof
            %h6 Proof
            %p
              Apply the previous theorem to the identity adjunction
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                      %mo ⇄
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C\rightleftarrows C
          %p
            Note that the !-modality obtained from the corollary is
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/idempotent+comonad"} idempotent
          %h3#RealizationInLinearHomotopyTypeTheory Realization in linear homotopy type theory
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+linear+homotopy+type+theory"} dependent linear homotopy type theory
            the “linear-nonlinear adjunction” is naturally identified (
            %a{:href => "#PontoShulman12"}> Ponto &amp; Shulman (2012), Ex. 4.2
            , see also
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Schreiber14"} Schreiber (2014), Sec. 4.2
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stabilization"} stabilization
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functor"} adjunction
            between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+types"} homotopy types
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+homotopy+types"} stable homotopy types
            (
            %a{:href => "#Riley22Thesis"}> Riley (2022), Prop. 2.1.31
            ), whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left adjoint
            (forming
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/suspension+spectra"} suspension spectra
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Σ
                      %mo +
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma^\infty_+
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Riley22Thesis"} Riley (2022), Rem. 2.4.13
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi Type
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B \,\colon\, Type
            to the linear
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+sum"} dependent sum
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ⋆
                    %mi B
                  %mi 𝟙
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ≔
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mi B
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo !
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mi B
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo *
                  %mi 𝟙
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \star_B \mathbb{1} \;\coloneqq\; (p_B)_! (p_B)^\ast \mathbb{1}
            over the monoidal unit in the context
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            %a{:href => "necessity+and+possibility#ModalQuantumLogic"} quantum modal logic
            this is forming the “linear randomization” of the given classical homotopy type (its “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motive"}> motive
            ”):
          #eq:MotivizationInLHott.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (2)
            %code.maruku-mathml
          %div{:style => "margin: -20px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/MotivizationInLHoTT-230825b.jpg", :width => "460px"}/
          %p
            Curiously, this homotopy-theoretic realization of the exponential modality (beware the little subtlety of whether or not to reflect onto
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+homotopy+types"} pointed homotopy types
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Σ
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo ∘
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mn ∞
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi Spectra
                  %mo →
                  %mi Spectra
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Sigma^\infty \circ \Omega^\infty
                  \\;\colon\;
                  Spectra \to Spectra
          %p
            has independently been argued to have properties of an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exponential+map"} exponential map
            in the context of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Goodwillie+calculus"} Goodwillie calculus
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "Goodwillie+calculus#GoodwillieDerivativeOfExponentialModality"} there
          %p#ComonadicityOfClassicalOverLinearHomotopyTypes
            Moreover
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Σ
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo ⊣
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma^\infty \dashv \Omega^\infty
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comonadic+adjunction"} comonadic adjunction
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simply+connected+homotopy+type"} simply connected
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+types"} homotopy types
            \&amp;lbrack;
            %a{:href => "#BlomquistHarper16"}> Blomquist &amp; Harper 2016 Thm. 1.8
            ;
            = succeed "&rbrack;," do
              %a{:href => "#HessKedziorek17"} Hess &amp; Kedziorek 2017 Thm. 3.11
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+homotopy+types"} pointed
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Σ
                      %mn ∞
                    %msup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma^\infty \Omega^\infty
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modales"} modales
            among
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+homotopy+types"} stable homotopy types
          %h3#antithesis_interpretation Antithesis interpretation
          %p
            In the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/antithesis+interpretation"} antithesis interpretation
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/affine+logic"} affine logic
            into
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/impredicative+mathematics"} impredicative
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+of+truth+values"} set of truth values
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+of+affine+propositions"} affine propositions
            by the set
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Ω
                    %mo ±
                  %mo ≔
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi P
                    %mo +
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi P
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi Ω
                  %mo ×
                  %mi Ω
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mo ¬
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi P
                    %mo +
                  %mo ∧
                  %msup
                    %mi P
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega_\pm \coloneqq \{(P^+, P^-) \in \Omega \times \Omega \vdash \neg (P^+ \wedge P^-)\}
          %p
            The !-modality is a function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo !
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi Ω
                    %mo ±
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi Ω
                    %mo ±
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} !:\Omega_\pm \to \Omega_\pm
            is defined for each affine proposition
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi P
                    %mo +
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi P
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi Ω
                    %mo ±
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (P^+, P^-) \in \Omega_\pm
            by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo !
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi P
                    %mo +
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi P
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≔
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi P
                    %mo +
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ¬
                  %msup
                    %mi P
                    %mo +
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} !(P^+, P^-) \coloneqq (P^+, \neg P^+)
          %p
          .num_theorem
            %h6 Theorem
            %p
              The !-modality is equal to the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multiplicative+conjunction"} multiplicative conjunction
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi P
                      %mo +
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi P
                      %mo −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (P^+, P^-)
              with itself
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo !
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi P
                      %mo +
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi P
                      %mo −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi P
                      %mo +
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi P
                      %mo −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi P
                      %mo +
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi P
                      %mo −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} !(P^+, P^-) = (P^+, P^-) \otimes (P^+, P^-)
            %p
          %p
          .proof
            %h6 Proof
            %p The multiplicative conjunction is given by
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi P
                      %mo +
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi P
                      %mo −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi P
                      %mo +
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi P
                      %mo −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi P
                      %mo +
                    %mo ∧
                    %msup
                      %mi P
                      %mo +
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi P
                      %mo +
                    %mo ⇒
                    %msup
                      %mi P
                      %mo −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∧
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi P
                      %mo +
                    %mo ⇒
                    %msup
                      %mi P
                      %mo −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (P^+, P^-) \otimes (P^+, P^-) = (P^+ \wedge P^+, (P^+ \Rightarrow P^-) \wedge (P^+ \Rightarrow P^-))
            %p
              Given two propositions
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi P
                      %mo +
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P^+
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi P
                      %mo −
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P^-
              such that
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ¬
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mi P
                        %mo +
                      %mo ∧
                      %msup
                        %mi P
                        %mo −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg (P^+ \wedge P^-)
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ¬
                      %msup
                        %mi P
                        %mo +
                      %mo =
                      %msup
                        %mi P
                        %mo +
                      %mo ⇒
                      %msup
                        %mi P
                        %mo −
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg P^+ = P^+ \Rightarrow P^-
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conjunction"} conjunction
              is
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/idempotent"} idempotent
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi P
                      %mo +
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi P
                      %mo −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi P
                      %mo +
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi P
                      %mo −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi P
                      %mo +
                    %mo ∧
                    %msup
                      %mi P
                      %mo +
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi P
                      %mo +
                    %mo ⇒
                    %msup
                      %mi P
                      %mo −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∧
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi P
                      %mo +
                    %mo ⇒
                    %msup
                      %mi P
                      %mo −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi P
                      %mo +
                    %mo ∧
                    %msup
                      %mi P
                      %mo +
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ¬
                    %msup
                      %mi P
                      %mo +
                    %mo ∧
                    %mo ¬
                    %msup
                      %mi P
                      %mo +
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi P
                      %mo +
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ¬
                    %msup
                      %mi P
                      %mo +
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo !
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi P
                      %mo +
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi P
                      %mo −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (P^+, P^-) \otimes (P^+, P^-) = (P^+ \wedge P^+, (P^+ \Rightarrow P^-) \wedge (P^+ \Rightarrow P^-)) = (P^+ \wedge P^+, \neg P^+ \wedge \neg P^+) = (P^+, \neg P^+) = !(P^+, P^-)
            %p
          %h2#modal_term_calculi Modal term calculi
          %p
            Girard’s original presentation of linear logic involved rules that explicitly assumed the presence of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo !
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} !
            on hypotheses or on entire contexts, such as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dereliction+rule"} dereliction
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weakening+rule"} weakening
            and
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/contraction+rule"} contraction
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi A
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi B
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ,
                      %mo !
                      %mi A
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi B
                  %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi B
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ,
                      %mo !
                      %mi A
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi B
                  %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ,
                      %mo !
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mo !
                      %mi A
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi B
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ,
                      %mo !
                      %mi A
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\Gamma, A \vdash B}{\Gamma, !A \vdash B} \qquad \frac{\Gamma \vdash B}{\Gamma, !A\vdash B} \qquad \frac{\Gamma,!A,!A \vdash B}{\Gamma, !A\vdash B}
          %p and “promotion”:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mo !
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi A
                    %mrow
                      %mo !
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mo !
                      %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{!\Gamma \vdash A}{!\Gamma\vdash !A}
          %p
            If this is translated into a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+deduction"} natural deduction
            style term calculus, the resulting rules are more complicated than those of most type formers. This can be avoided using
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+type+theory"} adjoint type theory
            with two context zones, one “nonlinear” one where contraction and weakening are permitted (and
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/admissible+rule"} admissible
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo !
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} !
            as a modality relating the two zones.
          %p
            Such a “modal” presentation of linear logic was first introduced by
            %a{:href => "#Girard93"} Girard 1993
            and then developed by
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Plotkin93"} Plotkin 1993
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Wadler93"} Wadler 1993
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Benton95"} Benton 1995
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Barber96"} Barber 1996
          %p
            This presentation also generalizes naturally to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+linear+type+theory"} dependent linear type theory
            = succeed "-modality" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo !
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} !
